/** RESTORE Days 84–97. Approved manuscript, paragraph breaks preserved. */
export const devotionals84to97: Record<number, { body: string[]; reflection: string[] }> = {
  "84": {
    "body": [
      "A person's real commitments show up in two places: the calendar and the bank statement. What we say we value is cheap. What we actually give our time and money to is the evidence. Nehemiah 10 turns the people's signatures into something that could be checked against both.",
      "The covenant begins with who is bound. \"The rest of the people—priests, Levites, gatekeepers, musicians, temple servants and all who separated themselves from the neighboring peoples for the sake of the Law of God, together with their wives and all their sons and daughters who are able to understand\" (10:28). Whole households are included, down to the children old enough to grasp it. Then they \"bind themselves with a curse and an oath to follow the Law of God given through Moses the servant of God\" (10:29).",
      "After that general promise comes the detail. The first item concerns marriage. \"We promise not to give our daughters in marriage to the peoples around us or take their daughters for our sons\" (10:30). This is the same concern we met in Ezra 9 and 10, and it needs the same care. Deuteronomy 7:3–4 gives the reason as religious, that such marriages would turn children away from the LORD. We should not turn it into a command for Christians about ethnicity.",
      "The second item is the Sabbath. \"When the neighboring peoples bring merchandise or grain to sell on the Sabbath, we will not buy from them on the Sabbath or on any holy day. Every seventh year we will forgo working the land and will cancel all debts\" (10:31). The second sentence is striking after Nehemiah 5, where the poor were being crushed by debts owed to their own countrymen. The same community that once pressed its poor into debt promises to cancel debts every seventh year.",
      "Then the money, and it is itemised. \"We assume the responsibility for carrying out the commands to give a third of a shekel each year for the service of the house of our God\" (10:32). The Law in Exodus 30:13 speaks of half a shekel. Scholars have suggested several reasons for the difference, including the economic hardship of the time, and we cannot be certain which is right. What is clear is that the people set a specific figure.",
      "The list continues with the things the house of God needs. \"For the bread set out on the table; for the regular grain offerings and burnt offerings; for the offerings on the Sabbaths, at the New Moon feasts and at the appointed festivals; for the holy offerings; for sin offerings to make atonement for Israel\" (10:33). They also draw lots to arrange when each family will bring wood for the altar (10:34), and promise the firstfruits of their crops (10:35) and the firstborn of their sons and animals (10:36). The firstborn sons were not sacrificed. The Law provided for them to be redeemed by a payment (Numbers 18:15–16).",
      "The tithes come last. They will bring the first of their ground meal and their produce to the storerooms, \"for it is the Levites who collect the tithes in all the towns where we work\" (10:37), and the Levites in turn will bring a tenth of the tithes up to the temple (10:38). It is an orderly system, built so that no one's share is forgotten.",
      "The whole section ends with a sentence that works as a summary: \"We will not neglect the house of our God\" (10:39). Notice what these promises are about. They are not dramatic vows of heroic sacrifice. They are regular, practical, repeated obligations: marriage choices, Sabbath habits, the seventh year, the annual levy, wood, firstfruits and tithes.",
      "That is a wise way to make a promise. A vague commitment to love God more can drift for years without anyone noticing. A promise attached to a weekly rest, a regular gift or a particular day can be checked. Specific promises are also easier to break, and chapter 13 will show where these ones failed.",
      "Jesus says something close to the principle. \"For where your treasure is, there your heart will be also\" (Matthew 6:21). The people of Nehemiah's day understood that devotion has to be put somewhere. The question is whether we have put ours where our words say it is."
    ],
    "reflection": [
      "If someone looked at your calendar and bank statement this month, what would they conclude you are committed to? What is one specific and regular commitment you could make this week, small enough to keep?"
    ]
  },
  "85": {
    "body": [
      "Remote towns often struggle to attract doctors and teachers. Governments offer incentives, and even then the jobs can stay vacant. Someone has to be willing to move away from the familiar for the sake of a place that needs people. Nehemiah 11 begins with that problem on a larger scale.",
      "Chapter 7 told us that the city was \"large and spacious, but there were few people in it\" (7:4). The wall was up, but Jerusalem was still thinly populated, and a wall around empty space is of little use. Now the solution is described. \"Now the leaders of the people settled in Jerusalem. The rest of the people cast lots to bring one out of every ten of them to live in Jerusalem, the holy city, while the remaining nine were to stay in their own towns\" (11:1).",
      "The leaders go first, as they did on the wall. The rest of the community uses lots, one family in ten, so that the burden of moving is shared by the whole people. Casting lots was a way of leaving the decision to God, and it also removed any suggestion that someone was being picked out by favour. It was fair, and it was costly. The tenth family would leave a home and fields in a familiar village for a city that still had many unrebuilt houses.",
      "Then comes the moment that is easy to miss. \"The people commended all who volunteered to live in Jerusalem\" (11:2). Not everyone who lived in Jerusalem was chosen by lot. Some volunteered, and the community noticed and honoured them. In a city that was dangerous, unfinished and demanding, someone chose to go. The text records nothing about their motives, only that they were commended.",
      "The chapter then lists names and numbers, and they are worth reading as a record of real people. There were men of standing from Judah and from Benjamin. The priests who carried on work for the temple numbered 822 (11:12). Their associates in other duties are counted too, as are the Levites in the holy city, who \"totaled 284\" (11:18), and the gatekeepers, \"172 men\" (11:19). The temple had entrances to guard, and someone had to watch them.",
      "Two small details stand out. One Levite, Mattaniah's descendant, is described as \"the director who led in thanksgiving and prayer\" (11:17). There was someone whose job was to lead the community in thanks. And the singers were the subject of a royal order that regulated their daily duties or provision (11:23). The verse probably refers to an instruction from the Persian king, though which king is debated. Either way, it is a small reminder that the worship of Jerusalem existed inside a larger empire.",
      "What does the chapter ask us to notice? First, that God's work depends on people being willing to live where the need is. Second, that this willingness was shared between those who were called by lot and those who offered themselves. Both were part of the plan. The call may come through the community, and the willingness has to come from the person.",
      "That is worth remembering for anyone who feels a nudge toward costly service. A call that arrives through other people is not less real than one that arrives in the heart, and the two often confirm each other. In Jerusalem, the lot and the volunteer together filled the city.",
      "There is also a point about what the city meant. It is called \"the holy city\" (11:1). Living in Jerusalem was not simply a move to a bigger town. It was moving to the place where God had put his name, and that is why the people's willingness mattered.",
      "The writer to the Hebrews says of Abraham that \"he was looking forward to the city with foundations, whose architect and builder is God\" (Hebrews 11:10). The people of Nehemiah's Jerusalem were settling in a real city with a real wall. But those who volunteered were also pointing toward a city that no wall of Nehemiah's could finally contain."
    ],
    "reflection": [
      "Where might God be asking someone in your church or family to go, serve or settle, for the sake of a place that needs people? What would it look like to commend those who say yes, as Jerusalem did?"
    ]
  },
  "86": {
    "body": [
      "The second half of Nehemiah 11 reads like a list of place names, and it is easy to skim. It is better to treat it as a map. The writer wants us to look beyond Jerusalem to the towns and villages where most of God's people were actually living.",
      "The list begins with the settlements of Judah. \"Some of the people of Judah lived in Kiriath Arba and its surrounding settlements\" (11:25), along with many other towns that are named in turn. Kiriath Arba is the old name for Hebron (Genesis 23:2), the town where Sarah was buried and where Abraham had bought a field. It is a small reminder that these ordinary places carry very old memories.",
      "The text then draws a boundary. \"So they were living all the way from Beersheba to the Valley of Hinnom\" (11:30). Beersheba lay far to the south, one of the traditional southern limits of the land, while the Valley of Hinnom is right beside Jerusalem. The returnees were not a single crowd in one city. They were spread through a stretch of country, and each village had its own inhabitants and its own ancestral land.",
      "Benjamin's towns follow, running north of Jerusalem. The chapter closes with one more practical note: \"Some of the divisions of the Levites of Judah settled in Benjamin\" (11:36). The Levites, who had no tribal territory of their own, are placed where the people needed them. Even the arrangement of the clergy is described as part of the geography of the land.",
      "Why does a book about a rebuilt wall spend so many verses on the countryside? There are at least two reasons. The first is that the restoration was never meant to be confined to a capital city. God had promised a land, and Nehemiah 11 shows the people living in it again, in the places their ancestors had known. After Nehemiah 9's confession that \"we are slaves today, slaves in the land you gave our ancestors,\" a list of towns where God's people are once again settled is a modest and real sign of grace.",
      "The second reason is that the book honours the ordinary. These places are not famous. Some of them appear nowhere else in Scripture. Their contributions are less prominent in the narrative than those of the wall-builders or the singers, and they are still named. The work of God is carried by people in unremarkable places.",
      "It also helps to remember how small this restored community was. The land described is a fraction of David's kingdom, and only Judah and Benjamin are mentioned. Those who lived there were the remnant of a remnant. The writer does not disguise that. He lists the towns, and in doing so he invites the reader to hold together how modest the restoration was and how real.",
      "That may be the best way to read a chapter like this. We could take it as proof that the restoration was small, and so lament. Or we could take it as proof that it was real, and give thanks. The honest answer is both. By the standards of David's kingdom, the people in the towns of Judah and Benjamin were an unimpressive sight, but they were living in the land God had promised, and the God who promised it had brought them back to it.",
      "Reading a list like this aloud can become a small act of prayer. Each place name stands for households who got up in the morning, worked the same ground their grandparents had, and kept the faith without anyone noticing. God knew each of them.",
      "The risen Jesus gives his disciples a commission that moves in the same direction as this chapter, only much further. \"you will be my witnesses in Jerusalem, and in all Judea and Samaria, and to the ends of the earth\" (Acts 1:8). The movement from the city to the surrounding land is the first step in a movement that does not stop."
    ],
    "reflection": [
      "Think about the ordinary places where you live and work. Who in them might God be using without anyone noticing? What would it mean to give thanks for those people by name this week?"
    ]
  },
  "87": {
    "body": [
      "Many old Anglican churches have a board on the wall listing the rectors who have served the parish, name after name and date after date. Most visitors walk past it. For those who belong to the church, it carries a quiet message: this ministry did not begin with us and will not end with us.",
      "Nehemiah 12 begins with a board of that kind. \"These were the priests and Levites who returned with Zerubbabel son of Shealtiel and with Joshua\" (12:1). The names that follow cover the first return, nearly a century before Nehemiah's day. The writer wants the reader to know that those who were serving in the rebuilt city stood in a line.",
      "The record then moves forward through the generations. It names the high priests who succeeded Joshua, one after another, and it carries on to those who served \"in the days of Eliashib, Joiada, Johanan and Jaddua\" (12:22). Eliashib is a name we have met. He was the high priest who rebuilt the Sheep Gate in chapter 3. The name Eliashib will appear again in chapter 13, where the integrity of Jerusalem's priestly leadership comes under serious scrutiny. Even here, the list quietly carries the family forward.",
      "Notice that the list also tracks the Levites. \"The family heads of the Levites in the days of Eliashib, Joiada, Johanan and Jaddua\" are recorded (12:22), and \"the leaders of the Levites were Hashabiah, Sherebiah, Jeshua son of Kadmiel\" (12:24). Priests and Levites are listed because worship in Jerusalem depended on continuous service. Worship cannot be left to chance, and it cannot be left to one generation.",
      "The chapter ends this section by setting everything in time. These priests and Levites were recorded \"in the days of Nehemiah the governor and of Ezra the priest, the teacher of the Law\" (12:26). It is a rare moment where the two men appear together, one the governor and one the teacher, in a single sentence. Each had his work, and the lists show that behind both of them stood many others.",
      "There is a small curiosity in this passage. The list of high priests runs down to Jaddua, and the reference to the days of Jaddua suggests that the record was updated after Nehemiah's own time. Scholars often take this as a sign that someone later added to the material. That does not weaken its worth. It means the record was kept alive by those who came after.",
      "What does all of this teach? First, that the work of God is a relay. No one runs the whole race. Each generation receives the baton and passes it on, and the book honours the names of those who carried it. Paul tells Timothy to do exactly that: \"the things you have heard me say in the presence of many witnesses entrust to reliable people who will also be qualified to teach others\" (2 Timothy 2:2).",
      "Second, that mortal servants are a fragile foundation. Priest follows priest because each one dies. The lists record faithful men, and they also record how many it took. Hebrews 7:23–25 draws a similar contrast between a long succession of mortal priests and Jesus, whose priesthood is permanent.",
      "Third, that names matter even when little else is known. We know almost nothing about most of the people on these pages. They appear as a line, a father's name and a task. Yet God inspired the writer to preserve them. The Bible does not measure value by how much we know about someone. A name in Scripture is a sign that God noticed, which is worth holding onto on days when our own service feels unseen.",
      "It can be moving to read such a list slowly and say each name aloud. They were people who served in the dark years, kept the daily work going, and handed it to someone else."
    ],
    "reflection": [
      "Who passed the faith on to you, and who might you be passing it to? Write down two or three names, and thank God for them in prayer."
    ]
  },
  "88": {
    "body": [
      "The wall that Sanballat mocked, that Tobiah said a fox could knock down, and that the builders constructed with swords at their sides, is now the setting for a parade. At the dedication of the wall, Nehemiah turns it into a place of worship.",
      "\"At the dedication of the wall of Jerusalem, the Levites were sought out from where they lived to celebrate joyfully the dedication with songs of thanksgiving and with the music of cymbals, harps and lyres\" (12:27). The text does not tell us exactly how long after the wall's completion this took place. What matters is that the wall was not simply finished. It was dedicated, set apart for God, as the Sheep Gate had been in chapter 3.",
      "Before anything else, there is a purification. \"When the priests and Levites had purified themselves ceremonially, they purified the people, the gates and the wall\" (12:30). The order matters. The leaders of worship are cleansed first, then the people, then the very structures. Even stones and gates are brought before God.",
      "Then Nehemiah arranges the procession. \"I had the leaders of Judah go up on top of the wall. I also assigned two large choirs to give thanks\" (12:31). One choir goes to the right, toward the Dung Gate, with Hoshaiah and half the leaders following. Ezra \"the teacher of the Law led the procession\" (12:36). The text says the musicians used \"musical instruments prescribed by David the man of God.\" The second choir \"proceeded in the opposite direction,\" with Nehemiah and half the people following (12:38).",
      "Picture it. Two columns of singers and instrumentalists are walking along the top of the wall in opposite directions, circling the city. The wall that had been broken down and burned is now a platform. Each group passes landmarks we know from Nehemiah's night ride, including the Fountain Gate and the Water Gate. The same stones that he had inspected in the dark are now crowded with people singing.",
      "The two choirs meet at the temple. \"The two choirs that gave thanks then took their places in the house of God\" (12:40). Then the final verse: \"And on that day they offered great sacrifices, rejoicing because God had given them great joy. The women and children also rejoiced. The sound of rejoicing in Jerusalem could be heard far away\" (12:43).",
      "That last sentence echoes something we read in Ezra 3. At the laying of the temple foundation, the weeping of the old men and the shouting of the young were so mixed that no one could tell them apart, and \"the sound was heard far away\" (Ezra 3:13). Here, at the wall's dedication, the sound that carries is rejoicing only. Something has changed since the earlier scene, and the writer lets us hear it.",
      "Notice also the reason given for the joy. \"God had given them great joy\" (12:43). Their joy was not simply the satisfaction of a finished building project. It was a gift. The people who had worked so hard on the wall are told that even their rejoicing came from God.",
      "The image is a good corrective to the idea that rebuilding is only about hard work. Nehemiah's wall was built with sweat, but it was dedicated with song. A Grand Final crowd can be heard from the street outside the stadium. The people of Jerusalem made something like that sound, and passers-by who heard it would have known without being told that something good had happened to those inside.",
      "The Letter to the Hebrews says that believers \"have come to Mount Zion, to the city of the living God, the heavenly Jerusalem\" (Hebrews 12:22). The dedication of the wall is a joyful foretaste of what that city will be like."
    ],
    "reflection": [
      "When did you last celebrate something God had done, out loud and with others? What would it look like to mark a recent answer to prayer with thanksgiving this week, rather than moving straight on?"
    ]
  },
  "89": {
    "body": [
      "A Sunday service rests on a great deal that no one sees. Someone has booked the building, set out the chairs, paid the electricity bill and organised the roster for who plays and who reads. When it all works, nobody notices. When one piece fails, the gathering suffers. Nehemiah 12 ends with the equivalent of a church office.",
      "\"At that time men were appointed to be in charge of the storerooms for the contributions, firstfruits and tithes. From the fields around the towns they were to bring into the storerooms the portions required by the Law\" (12:44). After the joy of the dedication, the writer turns to the practical arrangements that make joy sustainable. Someone has to collect, store and distribute what the people give.",
      "Then comes a line that tells us how things looked at that moment: \"for Judah was pleased with the ministering priests and Levites\" (12:44). The people are glad of their religious leaders and want them to be properly supported. It is a healthy picture of a congregation and its ministers, in which the people give gladly and the ministers serve faithfully.",
      "The priests and Levites, in turn, \"performed the service of their God and the service of purification\" (12:45), and the singers and gatekeepers did their work, following the pattern of David and Solomon. The writer takes us back to an older standard: \"For long ago, in the days of David and Asaph, there had been directors for the musicians\" (12:46). The structures of worship that the community is rebuilding are not new inventions. They are recoveries of what God had given through David.",
      "The next verse pulls together the whole picture. \"At that time, all Israel contributed the daily portions for the musicians and the gatekeepers. They also set aside the portion for the other Levites, and the Levites set aside the portion for the descendants of Aaron\" (12:47). Everyone gives, and everyone passes some on. The people support the musicians and gatekeepers and the other Levites, and the Levites support the priests. It is a chain of giving.",
      "This is an unglamorous passage, and we should not be embarrassed by that. Much of God's work is sustained by regular gifts, faithful administration and people who do their job without applause. Storerooms and rosters do not make a good sermon illustration, but they keep the singers singing.",
      "Paul makes a similar point to the churches of Galatia. \"Nevertheless, the one who receives instruction in the word should share all good things with their instructor\" (Galatians 6:6). Supporting those who teach and serve is a normal part of a healthy community. It does not mean that all ministry must be full time or paid, since Paul sometimes supported himself by his own work. It means that those who benefit from the work should be willing to share in it. Giving money and giving time are different ways of doing the same thing, which is sustaining the ministry so that the worship can go on.",
      "Yet the placement of this passage is sobering. The writer says \"at that time\" the arrangements were in place. The commitments of chapter 10 were being kept, the storerooms were managed, and the people were pleased. It is a high point. When we reach chapter 13, we will discover that these same arrangements fell apart in Nehemiah's absence.",
      "That is how ordinary systems work. They need to be rebuilt, and then they need to be maintained. A rota that is carefully designed in one year can lapse the next if no one is paying attention. The structures of worship are only as strong as the willingness of the people to keep them.",
      "A healthy church understands this. It does not treat the giving, the counting and the rostering as the dull part that has to be endured before the real worship begins. It sees them as part of the worship, because they keep the praise going week after week."
    ],
    "reflection": [
      "Who in your church quietly keeps things running: the one who sets up, counts the offering, writes the roster or locks up? How could you thank one of them this week, and what small part of the unseen work could you share?"
    ]
  },
  "90": {
    "body": [
      "Throughout Ezra and Nehemiah, the people of God have been separating themselves, building walls, guarding gates and keeping the foreigner at a distance. That was a real part of their calling, as the earlier days have shown. Ephesians 2 tells us what happened to the boundaries when Christ came.",
      "Paul writes to Gentile believers and asks them to remember. \"Therefore, remember that formerly you who are Gentiles by birth ... remember that at that time you were separate from Christ, excluded from citizenship in Israel and foreigners to the covenants of the promise, without hope and without God in the world\" (2:11–12). It is a bleak description, and it is accurate. Outsiders to Israel really were outside the covenant community that Ezra and Nehemiah laboured to protect.",
      "Then the turn. \"But now in Christ Jesus you who once were far away have been brought near by the blood of Christ\" (2:13). The distance has been overcome, and the means is the cross. Paul does not say that the Gentiles have made themselves acceptable. They have been brought near.",
      "The next verse is the heart of the passage. \"For he himself is our peace, who has made the two groups one and has destroyed the barrier, the dividing wall of hostility\" (2:14). We should hear the word \"wall\" against the background of this devotional. Nehemiah built a wall to protect God's people. Paul says that a different kind of wall, a dividing wall of hostility between Jew and Gentile, has been demolished by Christ. Many readers connect this image with the barrier in Jerusalem's temple that kept Gentiles from its inner courts, though Paul does not say so directly.",
      "How was it destroyed? \"By setting aside in his flesh the law with its commands and regulations\" (2:15). We need to be careful here. Paul is not saying that God's moral will no longer matters. Elsewhere he insists that the Law is holy and good. The passage concerns the Mosaic Law as the covenantal boundary between Jew and Gentile, a boundary marked by things like circumcision and the food laws. Christ has set that boundary aside, so that he might \"create in himself one new humanity out of the two.\" That is not a licence for lawlessness. Those united to Christ are still called to holiness.",
      "This happens through the cross. Christ's aim was \"in one body to reconcile both of them to God through the cross, by which he put to death their hostility\" (2:16). The peace between Jew and Gentile flows from their shared reconciliation to God. Both groups needed reconciling to him, and both are reconciled in the same way.",
      "The access that follows is the answer to the longing of this whole devotional. \"He came and preached peace to you who were far away and peace to those who were near. For through him we both have access to the Father by one Spirit\" (2:17–18). People who had once stood at a distance from the temple can now approach the Father directly.",
      "And then the verse that sets Ezra 9:2, Nehemiah 9:2 and Nehemiah 13:3 in a new light. \"Consequently, you are no longer foreigners and strangers, but fellow citizens with God's people and also members of his household\" (2:19). The category that once sat outside the covenant community is now brought in. This does not tell us that ancient Israel's boundaries were wrong. It tells us that they were serving something that Christ has now accomplished.",
      "The passage ends with a building. The household is \"built on the foundation of the apostles and prophets, with Christ Jesus himself as the chief cornerstone\" (2:20). \"In him the whole building is joined together and rises to become a holy temple in the Lord. And in him you too are being built together to become a dwelling in which God lives by his Spirit\" (2:21–22). The temple that Zerubbabel rebuilt pointed ahead to this greater dwelling place of God in his people, and the old boundaries that Nehemiah guarded no longer define who belongs."
    ],
    "reflection": [
      "Who do you find yourself keeping at a distance, whether from your church, your friendships or your family? What does it mean that Christ has torn down the wall and made you part of one household?"
    ]
  },
  "91": {
    "body": [
      "Anyone who rereads a favourite book years later knows the odd experience of finding sentences that seem new. The words have not changed. The reader has. Nehemiah 13 opens with a rereading of that kind, and it shows a community still learning what its own Scripture asks of it.",
      "\"On that day the Book of Moses was read aloud in the hearing of the people\" (13:1). The phrase \"on that day\" is loose, and we should not press it. The chapter gathers several episodes, and verse 6 tells us that some of them happened years later, after Nehemiah had gone back to the king. What we can say is that, at some point, the Law was read publicly again, as it was in chapter 8, and the people listened.",
      "What they found was a specific text. \"There it was found written that no Ammonite or Moabite should ever be admitted into the assembly of God, because they had not met the Israelites with food and water but had hired Balaam to call a curse down on them\" (13:1–2). This summarises Deuteronomy 23:3–4. Notice the reason given. The restriction is expressed in terms of ancestry, but the reason supplied is the nations' historical hostility towards Israel, not a claim that one ethnicity is inherently inferior. It concerns how those two peoples treated Israel on the way to the land: they withheld bread and water, and they hired a prophet to curse God's people.",
      "Then comes the response. \"When the people heard this law, they excluded from Israel all who were of foreign descent\" (13:3). There is something admirable here. Hearing the Law led straight to action, as it did in chapters 8 to 10. The people did not nod at Scripture and move on.",
      "There is also something that should slow us down. The law they had just heard named Ammonites and Moabites. The action reported is wider: \"all who were of foreign descent.\" The verse is one sentence long. It does not tell us who was affected, how the exclusion worked, or whether Nehemiah directed it. The narrator reports what the people did and offers no comment, and we should not read more into that silence than the text gives us.",
      "The wider Bible shows that the question is not simple. Ruth was a Moabite woman who bound herself to Israel's God and Israel's people, and the book of Ruth traces her family line to King David. Rahab, a Canaanite, is also remembered with honour. Matthew's genealogy of Jesus names both: \"Salmon the father of Boaz, whose mother was Rahab, Boaz the father of Obed, whose mother was Ruth\" (Matthew 1:5). The line that leads to the Messiah includes a Moabite.",
      "None of this means the people of Nehemiah's day were simply wrong, and we should be wary of tidy verdicts. They were trying to protect a fragile community whose faith had been eroded before, and they were responding to a text they had just heard. But the genealogy of Jesus tells us that God's purposes were always wider than any boundary the community could draw, and Ephesians 2, which we read yesterday, shows where those purposes were heading.",
      "It would be easy to take the wrong lesson from this passage. One mistake is to conclude that Christians should exclude people because an Old Testament community did. The opposite mistake is to conclude that the people's obedience was worthless. Neither follows. The better lesson is about how we respond. The people heard God's word and acted on it at once, and that is worth imitating. It is also worth remembering that any single command is best read within the whole story of Scripture, which is why we keep returning to the book. A single reading never finishes the job.",
      "That is why the Bible is not read once. A sermon series, a Bible study group, a yearly read-through each give the Word another chance to correct what we got wrong last time. Faithful reading is humble reading. It assumes there is more to see.",
      "This guards us against treating our current reading as final, and against giving up because the book is hard. The people of Jerusalem kept coming back to it, even when what they found was uncomfortable."
    ],
    "reflection": [
      "Think of a passage you once read one way and later understood differently. What corrected you? Where might you need to reread something familiar, slowly, and ask what you have missed?"
    ]
  },
  "92": {
    "body": [
      "Some of the most damaging changes in a community happen while the person responsible for it is away. A school, a church or a business runs well under strong leadership, and then someone is absent for a season. Nobody announces a decision to lower standards. A small exception is granted, and then another. Nehemiah 13 tells us what happened in Jerusalem while its governor was gone.",
      "\"Before this, Eliashib the priest had been put in charge of the storerooms of the house of our God. He was closely associated with Tobiah\" (13:4). Tobiah has been a familiar name for a long time. He was one of the men who mocked the builders in chapter 4, and chapter 6 showed that he had family ties and sworn allies inside Judah. A man who tried to frighten Nehemiah from outside now has a friend inside.",
      "Eliashib is probably the high priest who rebuilt the Sheep Gate in chapter 3, though the text does not say so directly, and we should hold that identification lightly. What matters is his position. He was responsible for the storerooms, and he used his authority to give Tobiah \"a large room formerly used to store the grain offerings and incense and temple articles and the tithes\" (13:5). The space that had been set aside for what belonged to God was handed over to a man who had opposed God's work.",
      "The text then explains why Nehemiah did not know. \"But while all this was going on, I was not in Jerusalem, for in the thirty-second year of Artaxerxes king of Babylon I had returned to the king. Some time later I asked his permission and came back to Jerusalem\" (13:6–7). That is about twelve years after he first arrived. The text gives no reason for his return to Persia, and it does not say how long he stayed. It simply tells us the arrangement was made in his absence.",
      "Nehemiah's reaction is vivid. \"I was greatly displeased and threw all Tobiah's household goods out of the room\" (13:8). Then he gave orders, \"and they purified the rooms, and I put back into them the equipment of the house of God, with the grain offerings and the incense\" (13:9). The sequence matters. First the intruder's things are removed. Then the rooms are cleansed. Then they are returned to their proper use.",
      "It is worth noticing that the problem was not an outside attack. The previous chapters were full of threats from enemies. This one is a favour granted by an insider. A priest, with good standing and a legitimate role, opened a door that the enemy could never have forced. Compromise often comes in the way a friend does, with a plausible reason and a pleasant face.",
      "We can say a few things from this. The first is that holy space matters. Rooms for tithes and offerings were set apart, and putting something else in them was more than a housekeeping error. The second is that leadership has to keep watching. The storerooms had been organised in chapter 12, and they still needed oversight. The third is that it is possible to be in an official role and still fail to guard what you have been given.",
      "Jesus acted in much the same spirit when he entered the temple in Jerusalem and drove out those who were buying and selling. \"Stop turning my Father's house into a market!\" (John 2:16). His disciples remembered that Scripture says, \"Zeal for your house will consume me\" (John 2:17). Nehemiah's anger at Tobiah's room was a small shadow of that zeal, and Jesus shows what it looks like when it is perfectly pure.",
      "There is a further point for us. Paul says that Christians are themselves God's temple: \"God's temple is sacred, and you together are that temple\" (1 Corinthians 3:17). The question of what we allow into the space that belongs to God applies to our lives and to our churches."
    ],
    "reflection": [
      "What has quietly moved into a space in your life that was meant for God, whether time, attention or money? What would it take to clear it out, and who could help you keep it clear?"
    ]
  },
  "93": {
    "body": [
      "If you have ever been the only one doing a job nobody else notices, you will understand the end of Nehemiah 13:14. After a stretch of unglamorous repair work, Nehemiah prays a short prayer that sounds like a tired sigh. It is also one of the most honest verses in the book.",
      "First, the problem. \"I also learned that the portions assigned to the Levites had not been given to them, and that all the Levites and musicians responsible for the service had gone back to their own fields\" (13:10). The people had promised in chapter 10 to bring the tithes to the storerooms. The text does not say why the portions stopped. Whatever the cause, the result was clear: those who served in the temple had to go and find their own food.",
      "We should be careful not to read too much into this. The verse does not say the Levites were lazy, and it does not say the people were hostile to them. It says what happened. When the support disappeared, the workers left, and with them went the singing, the gate-keeping and the daily service of the house of God.",
      "Nehemiah's response is direct. \"So I rebuked the officials and asked them, 'Why is the house of God neglected?' Then I called them together and stationed them at their posts\" (13:11). The question echoes the promise in chapter 10: \"We will not neglect the house of our God\" (10:39). The covenant words are being quoted back at the people who made them.",
      "The repair is practical. \"All Judah brought the tithes of grain, new wine and olive oil into the storerooms\" (13:12). Nehemiah appoints trustworthy men to oversee the storerooms and distribute supplies (13:13). The Levites could return to their duties because the support had been restored.",
      "Then the prayer. \"Remember me for this, my God, and do not blot out what I have so faithfully done for the house of my God and its services\" (13:14). Some readers hear self-righteousness in it, as though Nehemiah were presenting a claim. That is a possible reading, and it is worth admitting. But another reading is also available. Nehemiah is not boasting to a crowd. He is praying to God, after a discouraging return, and asking him to take notice of work no one else may thank him for.",
      "It is also worth noticing what the verse does not say. Nehemiah does not claim that his reforms were perfect, or that the people had thanked him. He brings the work to God and leaves the verdict there.",
      "The prayer teaches us two things. The first is that it is right to bring our small and hidden faithfulness to God. The Lord sees, and Scripture often says so. The second is that we should be careful what we expect God to say in reply. Nehemiah asks God not to \"blot out\" his deeds, and the language is about being remembered. It need not be read as a bid to earn a reward.",
      "The New Testament gives us a prayer with the same shape from a very different place. A criminal dying next to Jesus says, \"Jesus, remember me when you come into your kingdom\" (Luke 23:42). He has no good works to point to. He asks only to be remembered by the right person. And the answer he receives is better than anything Nehemiah asked for: \"Truly I tell you, today you will be with me in paradise\" (Luke 23:43).",
      "Nehemiah's prayer will come back at the very end of the book, and we will return to it on Day 97. For now it is enough to notice that his plea, \"Remember me\", is a cry that belongs to all of God's people. We want to be known and kept. Jesus answers that cry for those who ask."
    ],
    "reflection": [
      "What faithful work of yours feels unseen right now? Bring it to God honestly, as Nehemiah did, and then ask what it would mean to rest in being known by him rather than thanked by people."
    ]
  },
  "94": {
    "body": [
      "A rest day is hard to protect because it does not usually lose to a villain. It loses to opportunity. A customer is willing to pay. A deadline is approaching. A good reason appears every week. In Nehemiah 13, the Sabbath is under pressure in exactly that way, and Nehemiah's response is firm.",
      "\"In those days I saw people in Judah treading winepresses on the Sabbath and bringing in grain and loading it on donkeys, together with wine, grapes, figs and all other kinds of loads. And they were bringing all this into Jerusalem on the Sabbath. Therefore I warned them against selling food on that day\" (13:15). This was a breach of the promise in chapter 10: \"we will not buy from them on the Sabbath or on any holy day\" (10:31). Some of the people of Judah were working on the Sabbath, and the market was open.",
      "Traders from outside the community made it worse. \"People from Tyre who lived in Jerusalem were bringing in fish and all kinds of merchandise and selling them in Jerusalem on the Sabbath to the people of Judah\" (13:16). The text does not blame the Tyrians alone. Nehemiah turns to the nobles. \"What is this wicked thing you are doing—desecrating the Sabbath day?\" (13:17). The responsibility sits with the leaders of Judah, who had let it happen.",
      "Then he gives them a history lesson. \"Didn't your ancestors do the same things, so that our God brought all this calamity on us and on this city? Now you are stirring up more wrath against Israel by desecrating the Sabbath\" (13:18). He ties the present habit to the exile. The Sabbath was not a minor rule. It was part of the covenant that the nation's earlier history had broken.",
      "His solution is practical and firm. He ordered the city gates shut as evening shadows fell before the Sabbath, and he stationed some of his own men at the gates, \"so that no load could be brought in on the Sabbath day\" (13:19). When the merchants camped outside the walls, he warned them: \"If you do this again, I will arrest you\" (13:21). The text says that \"from that time on they no longer came on the Sabbath.\" Finally he sent Levites to guard the gates \"in order to keep the Sabbath day holy\" (13:22).",
      "We should be honest about what is happening. Nehemiah is using the authority of a governor to enforce a covenant on a community that has already sworn to keep it. That is not how the church is called to work. Christians disagree about how the Sabbath commandment applies today, and this passage cannot settle that question for us. It does not give us a licence to impose religious rules on neighbours.",
      "A modern reader may also feel the cost. Shutting the gates meant lost trade for the merchants, and probably for Judah's own farmers as well. Obedience here was not free, which is why the promises of chapter 10 were costly as well as specific.",
      "What the passage does show is how seriously God treats rest. The Sabbath is an act of trust. To stop working is to say that God, and not our output, keeps the world running. Mark records Jesus saying, \"The Sabbath was made for man, not man for the Sabbath\" (Mark 2:27). It was given to us for our good. And Jesus adds, \"So the Son of Man is Lord even of the Sabbath\" (Mark 2:28). He did not abolish rest. He claimed authority over it.",
      "Notice also that the rest in this passage was not a private matter. The commercial pressure came from a whole city, and the response needed gates, guards and a community that agreed to defend each other's rest. That is worth remembering. We find it much easier to rest when the people around us rest too."
    ],
    "reflection": [
      "What takes the place of rest in your week? What is one boundary, a gate you could shut, that would give you a regular day or half-day free of work and worry?"
    ]
  },
  "95": {
    "body": [
      "Some problems keep returning because they were never about a single event. A family argument surfaces every Christmas. A habit slips back after months of progress. Nehemiah 13 brings us back to a problem we met in Ezra 9, and it does so with a sharp reminder that fixing something once does not fix it forever.",
      "\"Moreover, in those days I saw men of Judah who had married women from Ashdod, Ammon and Moab\" (13:23). The places named are the same kinds of neighbours that the covenant in chapter 10 had in view. As we noted on Day 84, the promise in 10:30 was not to give or take daughters in marriage among the peoples around them. Here the promise had been broken.",
      "Then Nehemiah adds a detail about the children. \"Half of their children spoke the language of Ashdod or the language of one of the other peoples, and did not know how to speak the language of Judah\" (13:24). The text does not say what this meant for them in religious terms, so we should not state more than it says. But it is not hard to see the concern. Chapter 8 showed a community gathered around a book. Children who could not speak the language of that community's worship and Scripture would struggle to be part of it.",
      "Nehemiah grounds his concern in covenant faithfulness, not ethnic superiority. He appeals to Solomon. \"Was it not because of marriages like these that Solomon king of Israel sinned? Among the many nations there was no king like him. He was loved by his God, and God made him king over all Israel, but even he was led into sin by foreign women\" (13:26). The point is spiritual. Solomon's wives brought their gods with them (1 Kings 11:4), and the nation's wisest king was led away. As with Ezra 9 and 10, the concern is faithfulness to the LORD. It is not a timeless rule about ancestry, and it does not cancel what Ephesians 2 says about God's household.",
      "Now Nehemiah's behaviour. \"I rebuked them and called curses down on them. I beat some of the men and pulled out their hair\" (13:25). We should say plainly that this is hard to read. In Ezra 9:3, Ezra tore out hair from his own head in grief. Nehemiah tears out other people's. The narrator reports it without comment. Nothing in the text invites us to copy it, and nothing in the New Testament would let us. Reporting a hero's action is not the same as commending it.",
      "Christian readers should add one caution. Marriage is not made holy by matching backgrounds. What the passage guards is shared worship of the LORD in the home, which is why Paul can say a widow is free to marry anyone she wishes, \"but he must belong to the Lord\" (1 Corinthians 7:39). Paul also does not tell believers already married to unbelievers to leave: \"If any brother has a wife who is not a believer and she is willing to live with him, he must not divorce her\" (1 Corinthians 7:12).",
      "Notice what is missing from Nehemiah's story. He does not tell us that his efforts worked. The question in verse 27, \"Must we hear now that you too are doing all this terrible wickedness and are being unfaithful to our God by marrying foreign women?\", sounds like a man who is exhausted. The wall had been finished for years, the covenant had been signed, and the same sin was back.",
      "Solomon, the man \"loved by his God,\" fell anyway. Jesus once pointed to the crowd around him and said, \"now something greater than Solomon is here\" (Matthew 12:42). The comparison matters. A wise and well-loved king still failed, and Israel needed a king who would not fail. Malachi 2:10–16 will speak to this same problem in a few days.",
      "The honest lesson is that even a well-led, covenant-keeping community can drift back into old patterns. That is not a reason for despair. It is a reason to keep returning to God, who is not tired of us."
    ],
    "reflection": [
      "What old pattern keeps coming back in your life even after you have dealt with it before? Who could you ask to walk with you through it, not just for a week but for the long haul?"
    ]
  },
  "96": {
    "body": [
      "The book of Nehemiah ends in an unusual way. There is no victory lap, no summary of the city's glory, and no final song. The last page is a list of small reforms and a prayer. The ending is abrupt, and it is meant to be felt.",
      "\"One of the sons of Joiada son of Eliashib the high priest was son-in-law to Sanballat the Horonite. And I drove him away from me\" (13:28). The man who had opposed the wall from the beginning now has a marriage alliance with the family of the high priest. Sanballat's name has been in this book since chapter 2. Here he appears one last time, and he is close to the centre of the temple.",
      "Nehemiah's reaction is a prayer. \"Remember them, my God, because they defiled the priestly office and the covenant of the priesthood and of the Levites\" (13:29). The language of covenant is important. The priesthood was a covenant office, set apart to stand between God and the people. When the high priest's family compromised it, the damage was to the very structure that made worship possible.",
      "It is worth remembering where this family had stood. Eliashib the high priest opened the Sheep Gate in chapter 3, and in chapter 13 his household is again at the centre of the story, this time on the wrong side of it. The book is willing to say that leadership can be compromised, even leadership in the best position. That honesty is part of why we can trust its account of the good.",
      "Then comes a summary of his reforms. \"So I purified the priests and the Levites of everything foreign and assigned them duties, each to his own task. I also made provision for contributions of wood at designated times, and for the firstfruits\" (13:30–31). These are practical matters we met in chapter 10. Wood for the altar and firstfruits for the priests were promises the people had made, and Nehemiah is making sure they are kept.",
      "And the book closes: \"Remember me with favor, my God\" (13:31). That is the final line.",
      "Ask what the ending is telling us. It does not say everything was put right. Instead we are left with a governor praying, a priesthood under strain and a community that needs constant care. Ezra ended with a list of men who had married foreign women. Nehemiah ends with a high priest's grandson tied to Sanballat. Both books finish with a problem still in view.",
      "That is not a failure of the writing. It is the writing telling the truth. The restoration was real: a temple, a wall, a Law read aloud, a covenant sworn, a city that sang. But it was incomplete. The people could be moved to repent, and they could still fall back.",
      "The question the ending raises is who will do what Nehemiah could not do. A governor can purify the storerooms and shut the gates. He cannot change the heart. Even the priests, the men set apart to bring the people near to God, could fail. The people needed a priest who would not defile the office.",
      "The letter to the Hebrews says this directly. \"Such a high priest truly meets our need—one who is holy, blameless, pure, set apart from sinners, exalted above the heavens\" (Hebrews 7:26). We will spend more time with Hebrews in the final days of this devotional. For now, notice how Nehemiah's last pages create the longing that Hebrews answers.",
      "Meanwhile, the final prayer, \"Remember me with favor\", is a good place for any of us to end a day. It is brief, and it assumes that God is gracious. It is not a demand for a reward God owes him. It is a plea to be remembered with favour by the God whose grace he needs."
    ],
    "reflection": [
      "If your life's story ended on an unfinished page, as Nehemiah's does, what would you want to have prayed? Write a one-sentence prayer of your own that asks for God's favour and rests in it."
    ]
  },
  "97": {
    "body": [
      "When you finish a long journey, it helps to look back at the route. We have walked through Ezra and Nehemiah for ninety-seven days, from the decree of Cyrus to the last page of Nehemiah's memoirs. Today we pause at the final line, \"Remember me with favor, my God\" (13:31), and ask what the two books are telling us together.",
      "They tell a story of real restoration. A people who had lost their land returned to it. The altar was rebuilt, and then the temple, though the old men wept at the sight. A scribe named Ezra brought the Law to the city. A cupbearer named Nehemiah rebuilt a broken wall in fifty-two days under threat, with weapons kept close at hand. The Law was read aloud, the people confessed, a covenant was sworn, and the wall was dedicated with two great choirs. None of that was small, and none of it was an illusion.",
      "They also tell a story of restoration that is unfinished. Ezra ends with a painful response to covenant unfaithfulness. Nehemiah ends with the same problem returning, the temple compromised, the Sabbath neglected and the tithes forgotten. The people who swore to be different in chapter 10 had slipped by chapter 13. The writer lets the pages end there.",
      "Hold those two stories together. If we remember only the first, we will think that enough effort and enough reform can fix a people. If we remember only the second, we will conclude that nothing really changed. The books say both: God truly restored his people, and they still needed more than a wall.",
      "Notice also how often the books point beyond themselves. Haggai and Zechariah, whose ministry appears in Ezra 5, spoke of a future glory. The people confessed that they were \"slaves today\" (Nehemiah 9:36) in the land God had given. The covenant was kept in part and broken in part. Everything was waiting for something more.",
      "Notice what Ezra and Nehemiah give us that few other books do: a record of what returning from exile actually felt like. The enemies were real, the paperwork was real, and the weeping and laughing were real. The books do not pretend that obedience is easy or that renewal is a straight line. That honesty makes them a reliable guide for those of us who live between the first signs of renewal and its completion.",
      "That is where the final prayer fits. Nehemiah asked, \"Remember me.\" Throughout the book, he asked God to remember: to remember the covenant, to remember his enemies' deeds, to remember his own work. At the end he asks for favour. The whole book has been shaped by people asking God to look on them and act.",
      "The New Testament answers that cry in a song. When Zechariah, the father of John the Baptist, praises God, he says that God \"has come to his people and redeemed them,\" in order \"to show mercy to our ancestors and to remember his holy covenant\" (Luke 1:68, 72). God's remembering is not a vague kindness. It is his covenant faithfulness, and Luke says it is now being carried out in the coming of Jesus. Paul puts the same conviction in a single line: \"no matter how many promises God has made, they are 'Yes' in Christ\" (2 Corinthians 1:20).",
      "Over the next days we will meet Malachi, who speaks into this same period, and then the New Testament writers who show us how Ezra and Nehemiah's hopes are fulfilled. For now, we can end where the book does. The wall was built, and it was not enough. The temple stood, and it was not enough. The Law was read, and hearts still wandered. God was not finished.",
      "That is good news for people who also begin well and end unfinished. We do not need to be the last chapter. We are part of a story that God is still writing, and he is a God who remembers."
    ],
    "reflection": [
      "Looking back over these ninety-seven days, which moment has stayed with you most? What has God restored in you, and what are you still waiting for him to finish?"
    ]
  }
};
